WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:03.460
- [Narrator] Measuring only 820 feet long

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.460
and 200 feet wide, this sleepy island

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.950
was once a central location and stop-off

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.965
for a thriving logging industry.

00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:13.548
(gentle music)

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.870
- [Narrator] Sitting around one mile

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.760
outside of Rovaniemi City-the
capital city of Lapland-

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.613
is one of Finland's tiniest islands.

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:29.060
(soft electronic music)

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.370
The picturesque Kotisaari Island

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.663
is surrounded by the River Kemijoki.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.580
The river was once used
as a floating channel

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.420
by the log industry until 1980,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.810
after which the practice
of moving logs on water

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.333
was replaced by trucks.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.400
While no one lives on the island,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.210
it is accessible to visitors
by boat from May to October,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.903
with roughly 500 guests making
the trek to visit per year.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.930
To this day, the island
remains looking as it did

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.300
back in its prime.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.230
The only hint of modern
times is the old boathouse,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.650
which has been renovated
and turned into a tavern

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.720
for visitors to have a meal and explore

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.063
the lumberjack rest stop.

00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:18.566
(logo dinging)

